<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jibin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kipin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jibin was the Chinese name for a kingdom or region in the north-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, usually identified with Gandhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Chinese histories such as the Han shu record that a Saka king fled south across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a> to rule Jibin after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> displaced his people. The name continued in later Chinese records, though the territory it denoted shifted over time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jibin.jpg" alt="Jibin" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: トムル · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Regions_1st_century_BC(en).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
